# 6. LAYER RESPONSIBILITIES

## components/

Organize components by:

```text
role → feature
```

Example:

```text
components/admin/users/
components/staff/tickets/
components/public/requests/
```

Components should primarily render UI and receive data/actions through props.

They must not directly call APIs.

They must not directly use `fetch`.

They must not contain unnecessary business logic.

---

## components/ui/

Generic UI primitives only.

Examples:

```text
Button
Input
Dialog
Select
Table
Badge
Card
Dropdown
```

Rules:

* No business logic.
* No role logic.
* No feature-specific API calls.
* No domain assumptions.
* Reusable through props.
* Continue using the project's existing Radix/cva/Tailwind approach.

---

## components/shared/ and components/common/

Use for reusable building blocks that are shared across roles but are not generic UI primitives.

Rules:

* No role-specific authorization logic.
* No direct API calls.
* Prefer props over hidden business behavior.

---

## pages/

Pages are route-level components.

A page:

* Calls the appropriate hook.
* Connects data/state to UI components.
* Handles page-level loading/error/empty states.
* Coordinates feature components.

A page must NOT:

* Call `fetch`.
* Call `lib/*` API functions directly.
* Implement its own API client.
* Contain large reusable UI implementations.

Correct flow:

```text
Page
 ↓
Hook
 ↓
lib API
 ↓
Backend
```

---

## hooks/

Hooks mirror the role/feature structure.

Example:

```text
hooks/admin/users/use-admin-users.ts
```

Hooks are the only layer that uses React Query.

Use:

* `useQuery`
* `useMutation`
* appropriate React Query patterns

Hooks should connect React Query with pure `lib` functions.

Do not place raw API calls inside hooks.

---

## lib/

`lib/` contains pure application infrastructure and API functions.

It must not depend on React or React Query.

Feature APIs belong under:

```text
lib/<role>/<feature>/api.ts
```

Example:

```text
lib/admin/users/api.ts
```

API functions:

* Call the real backend.
* Use the shared API client.
* Follow backend request/response contracts.
* Do not contain React logic.
* Do not invent endpoints or fields.

---

## lib/api/client.ts

This is the shared API client.

Do not create another API client unless explicitly required.

All API requests should use the established client.

---

## layouts/

Use one shell per role.

Examples:

```text
admin-layout.tsx
public-layout.tsx
```

Layouts render the route outlet.

Do not place feature-specific business logic inside layouts.

---

## routes/

`route-config.tsx` is the single source of truth for the route tree.

`lazy-pages.ts` is the ONLY place where `React.lazy()` is called.

All route-level pages must be lazy-loaded through the established mechanism.

Do not create another routing system.

---

## providers/

Use one provider per concern.

Examples:

```text
query-provider
auth-provider
theme-provider
```

Providers are composed in:

```text
app-providers.tsx
```

Only `main.tsx` should directly mount the application provider composition.

---

## contexts/

Follow the existing context pattern:

```text
context object
+
useXContext hook
```

Do not introduce context for state that belongs in React Query or Zustand.

---

## store/

Zustand is for client-only UI state.

Examples:

* Sidebar open/closed.
* UI preferences.
* Temporary client-only interface state.

Do NOT store server state in Zustand.

Server state belongs to React Query.

---

## types/

Use strict TypeScript types organized by domain.

Examples:

```text
types/admin/user.ts
types/auth.ts
types/api.ts
```

Do not duplicate domain types unnecessarily.

Do not use `any` in domain code.

Prefer explicit types, unions, generics, and existing shared types.

---

## constant/

Use for application-wide constants.

Example:

```text
constant/routes.ts
```

Do not scatter duplicated constants throughout the application.

---

## motion/

Use the existing shared Framer Motion variants.

Do not create duplicated animation patterns unnecessarily.

---

## styles/

Follow the existing Tailwind architecture and CSS custom properties/design tokens in:

```text
styles/globals.css
```

Do not introduce an unrelated styling system.

### Brand identity — colors (mandatory, no exceptions)

This project has one fixed visual identity ("Forest"). Only these colors may be used anywhere in the UI (via the existing CSS variables in `styles/globals.css` — never hardcode hex values in components):

```text
Forest (primary brand)
  --syid-forest        #428177
  --syid-forest-dark   #054239
  --syid-forest-deep   #002623

Neutrals
  #ffffff
  --syid-charcoal      #3d3a3b
  --syid-charcoal-deep #161616

Gold / sand
  --syid-gold-light    #edebe0
  --syid-gold          #b9a779
  --syid-gold-dark     #988561
```

Rules:

* Do not introduce any new color, hex value, or Tailwind default color (e.g. `blue-500`, `indigo-600`, `red-500`) anywhere in the app.
* Always consume color through the existing CSS variables (`--syid-forest`, `--color-primary`, `--color-bg`, `--color-text`, etc.) or existing utility classes already defined in `globals.css`. Never inline a raw hex/rgb color in a component.
* If a new UI element needs a color role (e.g. an accent, a hover state), map it to the closest existing token/variable from the palette above. Do not invent a new shade unless it is a `color-mix()`/opacity derivation of an existing brand color, following the pattern already used in `globals.css`.
* Treat `styles/globals.css` as the single source of truth for color tokens. If a needed token does not exist yet, add it to `:root` (and `html.theme-dark` if a dark-mode variant is needed) using the Forest/gold/neutral palette — do not define it locally in a component or a one-off inline style.

### Brand identity — typography (mandatory)

The brand typeface is **Forest**, loaded via `@font-face` in `styles/globals.css` from `public/fonts/` (`HayyakumAllah-*` files: Light 300, Regular 400, Medium 500, Bold 700).

* `--font-family` is already set to use `'Forest'` first, with fallbacks. Do not override `font-family` in components; rely on `body`/`--font-family` cascading, or existing utility classes.
* Do not import or introduce any other font family (Google Fonts, other webfonts, etc.).

### Reuse existing styles before creating new ones (mandatory)

`styles/globals.css` already defines classes/utilities for most recurring UI elements (cards, tables, tabs, badges, pagination, form controls, buttons, dropdowns, tooltips, etc.), each already wired to the Forest/gold palette above, including dark-mode variants.

* Before creating a field/component/element, search `globals.css` for an existing class that already styles that kind of element (e.g. `.ui-card`, `.form-control`, `.ui-pagination`, `.org-tabs__link`, `.badge-status-*`, `.detail-grid__item`).
* If a matching class exists, reuse it as-is. Do not re-implement equivalent styling with inline Tailwind utilities or new hardcoded CSS.
* Every time a new instance of an already-styled element type is created anywhere in the app (a new table, a new card, a new tab bar, a new badge, etc.), it must use the same existing class/pattern — never a divergent one-off style for the same element type.
* Only add a new class to `globals.css` when no existing class covers the need. When adding one, follow the existing token-based approach (`var(--color-*)`, `var(--syid-*)`, `var(--space-*)`, `var(--radius-*)`) so it stays consistent with the rest of the file and both themes.

