# 15A. LANGUAGE — ARABIC ONLY, RTL ONLY (mandatory, no exceptions)

This application is Arabic-only. There is no i18n/localization system, and none should be introduced.

* `index.html` is fixed to `lang="ar"` and `dir="rtl"`. Never change these, never add a language switcher, never add English as an alternate locale.
* Every user-visible string in the entire app must be in Arabic: page titles, headings, labels, placeholders, buttons, links, menu items, table headers/cells, tooltips, `alt`/`aria-label`/`title` attributes, toast/notification messages, empty states, loading states, and all validation/error messages (including Zod/react-hook-form messages and API-error fallback messages).
* Do not introduce any new English UI text, ever — not even temporarily, not even as a "TODO" placeholder, not even for technical-sounding labels (e.g. write "جاري التحميل..." not "Loading...", "لا توجد بيانات" not "No data", "غير معروف" not "Unknown").
* Exceptions (must stay as-is, do not translate): the product/brand name itself where it appears as a proper noun, values that come verbatim from the backend contract (e.g. raw enum/status codes before they are mapped to an Arabic label), code identifiers, file paths, and third-party library defaults that cannot be overridden without a real i18n system (report these as a gap instead of hacking around them).
* Layout must work correctly in RTL: use logical CSS properties/utilities already used in the codebase (`margin-inline-start/end`, `border-inline-start/end`, RTL-aware Tailwind classes) rather than hardcoded `left`/`right` that assume LTR. Icons/arrows that imply direction (chevrons, back/forward) must visually point the correct way in RTL.
* When implementing or touching any component, page, hook, or validation schema, always output/keep the Arabic strings — never revert to or introduce English strings as part of an otherwise unrelated change.

